  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday night through Sunday)\nIssued at 231 AM EDT Mon Jul 20 2026\n\nThe mid to late week period this week maintains a mid-level and \nupper-level trough across the Mid-Atlantic and Northeast U.S. while \nsurface high pressure expands back over the region through the Wed-\nThu time frame. The previously mentioned drier air mass is also \nlikely to linger through this time period, which will keep rain \nchances lower and mostly in the 20-40% range. Highest chances will \nbe over Southwest Florida as easterly flow becomes re-established \nfor Wednesday and Thursday with the surface high expanding back over \nthe area. \n\nBy late Thursday and heading into Friday, guidance continues to \ntrack a westward moving trough that could exhibit some weak tropical \ncharacteristics. Nevertheless, ensembles and most individual members \nexhibit this trough maintaining a weaker form with no identified \nnear-surface circulation while either getting shredded apart or \nsucked into the trough pattern over the Mid-Atlantic. As a result, \nno major concerns are currently expected even if the system pushes \nacross the Florida Straits or far southern Florida like some of the \ndeterministic data suggests. Uncertainty still remains high, but at \nminimum PWATs are anticipated to rise back towards climatological \nnorms by late Thursday through Friday and next weekend, which will \nlead to rising rain chances. Highest chances each day are likely \nover interior regions as this will be where the sea breezes and \nother boundaries collide.\n\nHeat will be the other concern as temperatures are forecast in the \nmid to upper 90s each day through the end of this week along with \ndrier conditions through at least Wednesday and most of Thursday. As \na result, heat advisories may become necessary each day. Regardless \nif an advisory is issued or not, it is advised to continue to limit \nstrenuous outdoor activities and take action when you begin to \nexperience symptoms of heat exhaustion or heat stroke because \ntemperatures and heat index values will still be elevated.",
